{"id":"insider-leases","title":"City-funded nonprofits that rent from their own insiders","tier":"REDFLAG","question":"Which City-funded organizations told the IRS they pay rent to, or collect rent from, a board member, officer, relative or company they control?","finding":"6 City-funded organizations told the IRS they have a lease or rent arrangement with a board member, officer, founder or relative in which the organization is not described as the landlord, $956,301 over the returns on file; the largest is 18 Reasons with Sam Mogannam (board member), $187,245. 2 more collect rent from insiders ($4,060,378; a wholesale market whose merchant tenants sit on its board, and a hospital renting to its doctors), and 31 reported leases with related organizations they control or are controlled by ($113,661,853).","how":"Form 990 Schedule L Part IV (business dealings with interested persons) where the description is rent or a lease, and Schedule R Part V transactions coded as leases to or from a related organization, from the latest version of each return on disk (tax years 2020–2025). One amount per organization, counterparty and year.","limits":"Reporting the deal is what the law requires, and a board member who rents space to a charity at or below market does it a favour. Related-organization leases are routine where a nonprofit holds its buildings in a separate company. The returns do not say whether the rent is at market or whether City money pays it. City paid is everything the City paid the organization, not the rent.","columns":[{"k":"org","t":"Organization","type":"org"},{"k":"with","t":"Deals with","type":"person"},{"k":"relationship","t":"Relationship as reported","type":"text"},{"k":"what","t":"What for","type":"text"},{"k":"direction","t":"Which way the rent goes","type":"text"},{"k":"years","t":"Tax years","type":"text"},{"k":"total","t":"Reported, all years","type":"usd"},{"k":"latest","t":"Latest year","type":"usd"},{"k":"cityPaid","t":"City paid the organiza